## Questions

### Q1 — Topic 3.2

An internal research agent queries a document-management system through an MCP server. The
server authenticates the agent with a service credential that can read every repository. An
intern recently obtained content from the legal team’s restricted repository simply by
asking the agent. Lawyers use the same agent and legitimately need that repository. What is
the best fix?

A) Replace the service credential with a narrower one that excludes the restricted
   repositories.

B) Propagate the end user’s identity (a delegated, per-user token) to the MCP server so each
   request is authorised against that user’s own permissions.

C) Log every document access and review the log weekly for policy violations.

D) Add an output classifier that redacts content from documents labelled restricted before
   it is displayed.

### Q2 — Topic 3.2

A team connects its agent to a remote MCP server for a SaaS calendar. The agent only needs
to read colleagues’ free/busy status. The OAuth consent screen requests full-account scope:
read/write mail, contacts, calendar, and admin settings. A security review flags the
integration. What is the right response?

A) Accept the grant, since the agent’s tool list only exposes a free/busy lookup.

B) Accept the grant, but store the token encrypted at rest.

C) Accept the grant, but shorten the token lifetime to one hour.

D) Request only the minimal scope the capability needs (read-only free/busy), and if the
   server can’t be configured that way, don’t connect it as-is.

### Q3 — Topic 3.2 *(select TWO)*

A team’s MCP server configuration is checked into version control so every developer’s
Claude Code picks it up. Its env block contains the production database password in plain
text, and it has been in the repository for four months. Which TWO actions are most
important?

A) Rotate the exposed credential immediately, since it persists in the repository history.

B) Make the repository private.

C) Move the same password into each developer’s personal (user-level) config instead.

D) Replace the literal value with a runtime reference (an environment variable populated
   from a secrets manager) so no secret lives in the config file, and point developer
   configs at non-production credentials.

E) Add the config file to .gitignore going forward.

### Q4 — Topic 3.2

An operations agent has five read-only monitoring tools and one restart_service tool. All
six are implemented through a generic cloud-CLI wrapper that runs under a single
account-wide admin credential. In a red-team test, instructions planted in a log line caused
the agent to call a monitoring tool whose arguments, passed through to the CLI, deleted a
storage bucket. The team proposes adding human approval to restart_service. What is the best
fix?

A) Run each tool under a credential scoped to its function — a read-only role for the
   monitoring tools, a restart-only role for restart_service — so a read tool can’t perform
   writes whatever arguments it receives.

B) Add human approval to restart_service, as proposed.

C) Split into two agents, one holding the monitoring tools and one holding restart_service.

D) Sanitise log content for instruction-like text before the agent reads it.

### Q5 — Topic 3.3

A voice assistant’s SLA requires 95% of responses within 1.2 seconds. Current latency is 600
ms at p50 and 900 ms at p95. A new verification pass raises accuracy by 4 points, but its
latency is long-tailed: it adds about 400 ms at the median and pushes fleet p95 to about 2.4
s. Nearly all of the accuracy gain comes from two high-stakes intents (payments and address
changes), which make up about 10% of traffic. What should the architect do?

A) Adopt it: the median stays well within 1.2 seconds.

B) Reject it: any change that breaches the SLA at p95 is off the table.

C) Apply verification only to the two high-stakes intents where the gain is concentrated,
   then confirm fleet-wide p95 stays within the SLA.

D) Adopt it, and stream the response audio so users hear output sooner.

### Q6 — Topic 3.3

A research assistant runs a query rewrite (300 ms), then searches three independent indexes
one after another (400 ms each), then re-ranks (500 ms), then generates (1.6 s). The total
is 3.6 s against a 3.0 s p95 SLA. The team proposes dropping the re-ranker, which would cost
6 points of accuracy. What is the best change?

A) Query the three indexes in parallel instead of sequentially, saving about 800 ms while
   keeping the re-ranker.

B) Drop the re-ranker, as proposed.

C) Switch generation to a smaller model to save time.

D) Add a response cache for repeated queries.

### Q7 — Topic 3.3

An internal analyst tool produces long reports, and full generation takes about 25 seconds.
There is no SLA on total time, and accuracy is the priority. Telemetry shows users abandon
after about 8 seconds of staring at a blank screen. The team proposes a smaller model that
finishes in 8 seconds, but evals show it loses 11 points of accuracy. What is the best
response?

A) Switch to the smaller model, as proposed.

B) Cap max output tokens so reports finish within 8 seconds.

C) Pre-generate reports overnight for the most common requests.

D) Stream output as it’s generated and show progress indicators, keeping the accurate model.
   The problem is perceived latency, not total time.

### Q8 — Topic 3.3

A fraud-scoring API returns JSON containing a decision, reason codes, and a 150-word
explanation for human analysts. The checkout service calling it enforces an 800 ms hard
timeout set by the payment partner. Current p95 is 1.4 s, most of it spent generating the
explanation. The team proposes enabling streaming. What is the best change?

A) Enable streaming so the response starts arriving sooner.

B) Return only the compact decision and reason codes synchronously, and generate the analyst
   explanation asynchronously afterwards.

C) Negotiate the timeout up to 1.5 seconds.

D) Switch the whole response to the smallest model.

### Q9 — Topic 3.5

Earnings-call transcripts are chunked by speaker turn. A chunk reading “Revenue in the
segment grew 12%, driven by enterprise renewals” retrieves poorly for “How did Acme’s cloud
segment perform in Q3 2025?”. The chunk never names the company, the quarter, or which
segment “the segment” refers to; that context is established earlier in the call. What is
the best indexing change?

A) Use larger chunks (whole transcript sections) so more context travels with each passage.

B) Add document-level metadata filters for company and quarter at retrieval time.

C) Before embedding, prepend each chunk with a short generated context stating the company,
   the quarter, and which segment or topic the passage discusses.

D) Expand user queries with synonyms for “revenue” and “growth.”

### Q10 — Topic 3.5

An enterprise RAG assistant indexes a document platform using a crawler that runs with admin
access. Answers occasionally quote HR compensation documents to ordinary employees. The
current mitigation is a post-generation filter that suppresses answers citing documents the
user can’t access. However, uncited content still shapes answers. What is the best fix?

A) Keep the post-generation filter, and require a citation for every sentence so nothing
   uncited slips through.

B) Exclude HR documents from the index entirely.

C) Build a separate index for each department.

D) Store each chunk’s source permissions as metadata at index time, keep them synced with
   the source ACLs, and filter retrieval by the requesting user’s identity before anything
   reaches the model.

### Q11 — Topic 3.5

To save cost, a team switched to a new embedding model for newly added documents only. The
existing 800,000 chunks keep their old embeddings, and queries are now embedded with the new
model. Retrieval of older documents has almost vanished. What explains this, and what is the
fix?

A) Vectors from different embedding models live in different, incompatible spaces, so
   similarity between new-model queries and old-model chunks is meaningless. Re-embed the
   whole corpus, ideally into a parallel index, then cut over.

B) The vector store applies recency weighting that penalises older chunks; disable it.

C) The new model produces vectors of a different magnitude; normalising all vectors fixes
   it.

D) The similarity threshold is too high for older documents; lower it for them.

### Q12 — Topic 3.5 *(select TWO)*

A support knowledge base mixes two content types: an FAQ of short question–answer pairs, and
long troubleshooting procedures with numbered steps. Under uniform 400-token chunking,
retrieval returns step 4 of a procedure without steps 1–3, and FAQ answers arrive separated
from the questions they answer. Which TWO indexing changes are best?

A) Uniform 256-token chunks with 50% overlap across all content.

B) Chunk the FAQ as exactly one chunk per question–answer pair.

C) Increase top-k to 30 so the missing steps are probably retrieved as well.

D) Strip the step numbering from procedures so each step reads as standalone text.

E) Index procedures in small chunks for matching, but return the full parent procedure
   whenever any of its chunks matches.

### Q13 — Topic 3.6

A sales assistant answers questions such as “total Q3 revenue for EMEA enterprise accounts
with contracts over $1M.” The data sits in a 2-million-row warehouse table, and questions
combine arbitrary filters. The current design embeds each row as text and retrieves the top
matches. Answers contain wrong totals. What is the best retrieval strategy?

A) Increase top-k to 500 rows so more of the relevant data is summed.

B) Embed pre-computed aggregate summaries (revenue by region and quarter).

C) Have the model generate a SQL query, executed through a read-only, validated query tool
   against the warehouse, and then explain the result.

D) Move to a larger model with a longer context window so more rows fit.

### Q14 — Topic 3.6

A manufacturer’s engineering assistant receives the question: “Which suppliers of components
used in our recalled X200 model also supply parts for the Y400?” Single-shot retrieval
returns passages about the X200 recall itself. Answering needs three linked lookups: the
X200’s components, those components’ suppliers, and which of those suppliers appear on the
Y400 supplier list. What is the best strategy?

A) Switch to hybrid keyword and semantic retrieval.

B) Decompose the question into dependent sub-queries — X200 components, then their
   suppliers, then overlap with the Y400 list — retrieving iteratively using each step’s
   results.

C) Add a re-ranker so the most relevant passages rise to the top.

D) Increase top-k so passages about components and suppliers are retrieved as well.

### Q15 — Topic 3.6

A retail assistant’s users send conversational follow-ups such as “what about the cheaper
one?” and “is that quieter?”. Retrieval on the raw follow-up text returns irrelevant
documents, even though the conversation makes the meaning obvious. What is the best change?

A) Use the entire conversation history as the search query.

B) Ask users to rephrase follow-ups as complete questions.

C) Switch to hybrid retrieval so keywords in follow-ups match more reliably.

D) Before retrieval, rewrite each follow-up into a standalone search query using the
   conversation context (e.g. “noise level of the Aria S2 dishwasher”).

### Q16 — Topic 3.6

A legal research tool uses hybrid first-stage retrieval. The relevant passage is in the top
50 candidates 94% of the time, but in the top 5 only 58% of the time. Generation uses the
top 5. Passages are long (about 2,000 tokens each), and an experiment feeding all 50 to the
model reduced accuracy because of distracting near-miss passages. Research runs
asynchronously, so the latency budget is generous. What is the best change?

A) Upgrade the embedding model to improve first-stage retrieval.

B) Feed the top 20 passages to the model as a middle ground.

C) Add a re-ranking stage that scores the top 50 candidates against the query and passes the
   best 5 to generation.

D) Re-chunk into smaller passages so more of them fit in the top 5.

### Q17 — Topic 3.7

A developer team uses Claude Code in their terminals and wants the agent to open pull
requests, read issues, and inspect cloud resources. Every developer already has the GitHub
