## Answer Key & Rationale

**Q1 — B.** This is a confused-deputy problem: the agent acts with its own broad authority
on behalf of users with narrower authority. Authorisation must evaluate the requesting user.
(A: closes the leak by also locking out the lawyers who need access, since a single agent
identity can’t express per-user rights. D: the content has already been retrieved and shaped
the answer, protection depends on labels being complete, and it would also block lawyers. C:
detects the problem after the fact instead of preventing it.)

**Q2 — D.** Least privilege applies at the credential level, because the token’s power is
the real blast radius. The tools the agent happens to expose don’t bound it. (A: the
strongest trap — a narrow tool surface doesn’t limit what a compromised server, a new tool
version, or an injection-driven call can do with a full-account token. B and C: sensible
hygiene that shrinks exposure windows and theft risk, but leaves a token that can still do
everything.)

**Q3 — A, D.** Treat the secret as compromised and remove secrets from configuration by
design. Rotation handles the past exposure; runtime secret references and non-production
credentials prevent the next one. (E: stops future commits but leaves history intact and
breaks the shared-config benefit. B: narrows the audience, but every developer and every
clone still holds a production password. C: spreads the same production secret onto every
laptop.)

**Q4 — A.** The damage came through a “read” tool carrying admin power. Scoping credentials
per tool makes each tool physically incapable of exceeding its purpose. (B: a real control,
but on the tool that wasn’t the attack path. C: the monitoring agent still runs every call
as admin. D: reasonable defence in depth, but it filters surface form, and a boundary can’t
depend on recognising every injection.)

**Q5 — C.** When a latency cost is paid everywhere but the benefit is concentrated, apply
the change selectively and re-measure against the SLA’s actual percentile. (B: correctly
rejects the full rollout but throws away the targeted option. A: measures the median against
an SLA defined at p95. D: verification has to finish before the answer can be spoken, so
streaming can’t start ahead of it.)

**Q6 — A.** Before trading accuracy for latency, look for latency the pipeline structure is
wasting. Independent stages running serially is free budget, and recovering it brings the
total to about 2.8 s. (B: pays with accuracy for time that could be recovered for nothing.
C: an unevaluated accuracy risk. D: research queries rarely repeat, so the hit rate is
unknown and probably low.)

**Q7 — D.** Match the lever to the kind of latency problem. Users abandon because nothing is
happening, not because 25 seconds breaks a requirement, so a perceived-latency fix preserves
accuracy. (A and B: spend accuracy or completeness on a constraint that doesn’t exist. C: a
real tactic for predictable requests, but it helps only the common subset and leaves the
blank-screen problem everywhere else.)

**Q8 — B.** A machine consumer needs the complete payload inside the deadline, so the fix
must reduce actual latency. Moving the part the caller doesn’t need off the critical path
does that. (A: the right lever for a human reader, as in the analyst tool, but useless when
checkout needs complete JSON. C: the timeout is set by the payment partner. D: an
unevaluated accuracy risk on the decision itself, when the latency is in the explanation.)

**Q9 — C.** Contextual chunk enrichment restores the situating information that chunking
removed, so each chunk is retrievable on its own. (B: the closest candidate — it resolves
company and quarter, but a document-level field can’t say which segment this passage is
about. A: dilutes the embedding and still may not reach back to where the segment was named.
D: expands the wrong side, since the query was already specific.)

**Q10 — D.** Access control has to happen before content reaches the model. Once a chunk is
in context it can influence the answer whether cited or not. (A: tightens a check that runs
after the exposure has already happened. B: overcorrects and removes content HR staff
legitimately need. C: coarse, because permissions aren’t shaped like departments and
cross-team documents break it.)

**Q11 — A.** An index is only coherent if every vector, including every query vector, comes
from the same model. Mixing models silently breaks similarity. (C: normalisation equalises
length, not meaning, and spaces don’t align even at the same dimensionality. D: loosens a
threshold on scores that are noise. B: invents a mechanism the scenario doesn’t support —
the collapse lines up exactly with the model switch.)

**Q12 — B, E.** Chunk along each content type’s natural unit. A Q&A pair is atomic, and a
procedure should be matched at fine grain but delivered whole. (A: overlap softens
boundaries but still cuts through pairs and procedures, just less abruptly. C: hopes the
right fragments arrive in the right order. D: removes the structure that tells the model a
step is part of a sequence.)

**Q13 — C.** Aggregations over structured data need an exact query engine. Similarity search
returns a sample, not a complete filtered set. (B: works when the question space is known in
advance, but arbitrary filter combinations will miss the pre-computed cuts. A and D: still a
sample of rows, summed by a language model.)

**Q14 — B.** In a multi-hop question, later lookups depend on the answers to earlier ones,
and no single query embedding can express that chain. (C: improves ordering within one
retrieval, but the needed passages aren’t candidates for this query at all. A: better
matching on the same underspecified query. D: widens the net around the wrong centre.)

**Q15 — D.** Query rewriting resolves references against the conversation so the retriever
gets a self-contained query. (A: includes the context, but also every stale topic, which
dilutes the embedding. C: there are no useful keywords in “the cheaper one.” B: pushes the
system’s job onto users.)

**Q16 — C.** High recall with low precision in the top few means the problem is ranking, not
retrieval. A second-stage re-ranker fixes exactly that, and the latency budget absorbs it.
(A: improves the stage that is already finding the passage 94% of the time. B: a hedge that
reintroduces the distraction the experiment measured. D: changes the units without fixing
the ordering.)

**Q17 — A.** When the environment has a shell, the CLIs are authenticated, and the model
already knows them, CLI integration is the cheapest correct option. Governance comes from
permission rules rather than a wrapping layer. (B: the right call for cross-application
reuse or shell-less environments, but here it’s maintenance with little benefit. C: rebuilds
what the CLIs already provide. D: adds an autonomous counterpart where no negotiation or
coordination is needed.)

**Q18 — B.** This is the inverse of the developer-terminal case. With untrusted end users
and multiple tenants, free-text command composition is an injection surface. A typed schema
gives validation and a clean place to attach tenant auth. (A: a regex over shell strings is
a famously leaky boundary. C: isolates tenants from each other but not the tenant from the
injection surface. D: no negotiation is involved; this is a single well-defined write.)

**Q19 — C.** The deciding question is whether the other side needs to make decisions or just
provide data. A data read is a tool or resource need, whatever the org chart looks like. (A:
over-generalises the cross-organisation case, where each side genuinely keeps its own agent
and autonomy. B: bypasses the API’s business logic and access control. D: manual, stale, and
unaudited.)

**Q20 — C, D.** CLI access is governed by bounding what may be invoked and what the
invocation is allowed to do. The allowlist bounds the commands; scoped credentials cap the
blast radius if something slips through. (B: advisory, and unattended CI has no one to catch
it. A: an overcorrection that abandons the chosen approach rather than governing it. E:
detective, and a month late.)
